.SS "Installation and Usage"
Prerequisites: \fRNode\&.js\fP (\fR^18\&.18\&.0\fP, \fR^20\&.9\&.0\fP, or \fR>=21\&.1\&.0\fP) built with SSL support\&. (If you are using an official Node\&.js distribution, SSL is always built in\&.)

.PP
You can install and configure ESLint using this command:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm init @eslint/config@latest
.fi
.PP

.PP
After that, you can run ESLint on any file or directory like this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npx eslint yourfile\&.js
.fi
.PP
.SS "pnpm Installation"
To use ESLint with pnpm, we recommend setting up a \fR\&.npmrc\fP file with at least the following settings:

.PP
.PP
.nf
auto\-install\-peers=true
node\-linker=hoisted
.fi
.PP

.PP
This ensures that pnpm installs dependencies in a way that is more compatible with npm and is less likely to produce errors\&.
.SS "Configuration"
You can configure rules in your \fReslint\&.config\&.js\fP files as in this example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { defineConfig } from "eslint/config";

export default defineConfig([
    {
        files: ["**/*\&.js", "**/*\&.cjs", "**/*\&.mjs"],
        rules: {
            "prefer\-const": "warn",
            "no\-constant\-binary\-expression": "error",
        },
    },
]);
.fi
.PP

.PP
The names \fR"prefer-const"\fP and \fR"no-constant-binary-expression"\fP are the names of \fRrules\fP in ESLint\&. The first value is the error level of the rule and can be one of these values:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"off"\fP or \fR0\fP - turn the rule off
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"warn"\fP or \fR1\fP - turn the rule on as a warning (doesn't affect exit code)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"error"\fP or \fR2\fP - turn the rule on as an error (exit code will be 1)
.PP

.PP
The three error levels allow you fine-grained control over how ESLint applies rules (for more configuration options and details, see the \fRconfiguration docs\fP)\&.
.SS "Version Support"
The ESLint team provides ongoing support for the current version and six months of limited support for the previous version\&. Limited support includes critical bug fixes, security issues, and compatibility issues only\&.

.PP
ESLint offers commercial support for both current and previous versions through our partners, \fRTidelift\fP and \fRHeroDevs\fP\&.

.PP
See \fRVersion Support\fP for more details\&.
.SS "Code of Conduct"
ESLint adheres to the \fROpenJS Foundation Code of Conduct\fP\&.
.SS "Filing Issues"
Before filing an issue, please be sure to read the guidelines for what you're reporting:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRBug Report\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRPropose a New Rule\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRProposing a Rule Change\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRRequest a Change\fP
.PP
.SS "Frequently Asked Questions"
.SS "Does ESLint support JSX?"
Yes, ESLint natively supports parsing JSX syntax (this must be enabled in \fRconfiguration\fP)\&. Please note that supporting JSX syntax \fIis not\fP the same as supporting React\&. React applies specific semantics to JSX syntax that ESLint doesn't recognize\&. We recommend using \fReslint-plugin-react\fP if you are using React and want React semantics\&.
.SS "Does Prettier replace ESLint?"
No, ESLint and Prettier have different jobs: ESLint is a linter (looking for problematic patterns) and Prettier is a code formatter\&. Using both tools is common, refer to \fRPrettier's documentation\fP to learn how to configure them to work well with each other\&.
.SS "What ECMAScript versions does ESLint support?"
ESLint has full support for ECMAScript 3, 5, and every year from 2015 up until the most recent stage 4 specification (the default)\&. You can set your desired ECMAScript syntax and other settings (like global variables) through \fRconfiguration\fP\&.
.SS "What about experimental features?"
ESLint's parser only officially supports the latest final ECMAScript standard\&. We will make changes to core rules in order to avoid crashes on stage 3 ECMAScript syntax proposals (as long as they are implemented using the correct experimental ESTree syntax)\&. We may make changes to core rules to better work with language extensions (such as JSX, Flow, and TypeScript) on a case-by-case basis\&.

.PP
In other cases (including if rules need to warn on more or fewer cases due to new syntax, rather than just not crashing), we recommend you use other parsers and/or rule plugins\&. If you are using Babel, you can use \fR@babel/eslint-parser\fP and \fR@babel/eslint-plugin\fP to use any option available in Babel\&.

.PP
Once a language feature has been adopted into the ECMAScript standard (stage 4 according to the \fRTC39 process\fP), we will accept issues and pull requests related to the new feature, subject to our \fRcontributing guidelines\fP\&. Until then, please use the appropriate parser and plugin(s) for your experimental feature\&.
.SS "Which Node\&.js versions does ESLint support?"
ESLint updates the supported Node\&.js versions with each major release of ESLint\&. At that time, ESLint's supported Node\&.js versions are updated to be:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
The most recent maintenance release of Node\&.js
.PP
.IP "1." 4
The lowest minor version of the Node\&.js LTS release that includes the features the ESLint team wants to use\&.
.PP
.IP "1." 4
The Node\&.js Current release
.PP

.PP
ESLint is also expected to work with Node\&.js versions released after the Node\&.js Current release\&.

.PP
Refer to the \fRQuick Start Guide\fP for the officially supported Node\&.js versions for a given ESLint release\&.
.SS "Where to ask for help?"
Open a \fRdiscussion\fP or stop by our \fRDiscord server\fP\&.
.SS "Why doesn't ESLint lock dependency versions?"
Lock files like \fRpackage-lock\&.json\fP are helpful for deployed applications\&. They ensure that dependencies are consistent between environments and across deployments\&.

.PP
Packages like \fReslint\fP that get published to the npm registry do not include lock files\&. \fRnpm install eslint\fP as a user will respect version constraints in ESLint's \fRpackage\&.json\fP\&. ESLint and its dependencies will be included in the user's lock file if one exists, but ESLint's own lock file would not be used\&.

.PP
We intentionally don't lock dependency versions so that we have the latest compatible dependency versions in development and CI that our users get when installing ESLint in a project\&.

.PP
The Twilio blog has a \fRdeeper dive\fP to learn more\&.
.SS "Releases"
We have scheduled releases every two weeks on Friday or Saturday\&. You can follow a \fRrelease issue\fP for updates about the scheduling of any particular release\&.
.SS "Security Policy"
ESLint takes security seriously\&. We work hard to ensure that ESLint is safe for everyone and that security issues are addressed quickly and responsibly\&. Read the full \fRsecurity policy\fP\&.
.SS "Semantic Versioning Policy"
ESLint follows \fRsemantic versioning\fP\&. However, due to the nature of ESLint as a code quality tool, it's not always clear when a minor or major version bump occurs\&. To help clarify this for everyone, we've defined the following semantic versioning policy for ESLint:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Patch release (intended to not break your lint build)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A bug fix in a rule that results in ESLint reporting fewer linting errors\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A bug fix to the CLI or core (including formatters)\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Improvements to documentation\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Non-user-facing changes such as refactoring code, adding, deleting, or modifying tests, and increasing test coverage\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Re-releasing after a failed release (i\&.e\&., publishing a release that doesn't work for anyone)\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Minor release (might break your lint build)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A bug fix in a rule that results in ESLint reporting more linting errors\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A new rule is created\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A new option to an existing rule that does not result in ESLint reporting more linting errors by default\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A new addition to an existing rule to support a newly-added language feature (within the last 12 months) that will result in ESLint reporting more linting errors by default\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
An existing rule is deprecated\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A new CLI capability is created\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
New capabilities to the public API are added (new classes, new methods, new arguments to existing methods, etc\&.)\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A new formatter is created\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fReslint:recommended\fP is updated and will result in strictly fewer linting errors (e\&.g\&., rule removals)\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Major release (likely to break your lint build)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fReslint:recommended\fP is updated and may result in new linting errors (e\&.g\&., rule additions, most rule option updates)\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
A new option to an existing rule that results in ESLint reporting more linting errors by default\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
An existing formatter is removed\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Part of the public API is removed or changed in an incompatible way\&. The public API includes:
.IP "    \(bu" 6
Rule schemas
.IP "    \(bu" 6
Configuration schema
.IP "    \(bu" 6
Command-line options
.IP "    \(bu" 6
Node\&.js API
.IP "    \(bu" 6
Rule, formatter, parser, plugin APIs
.PP

.PP

.PP

.PP
According to our policy, any minor update may report more linting errors than the previous release (ex: from a bug fix)\&. As such, we recommend using the tilde (\fR~\fP) in \fRpackage\&.json\fP e\&.g\&. \fR"eslint": "~3\&.1\&.0"\fP to guarantee the results of your builds\&.
